[
  {
    "id": "example-home-theatre-activity-home-theatre-activity-example",
    "type": "comment",
    "name": "Home theatre activity example",
    "info": "The state contract turns a request on home/<area>/theatre/request, which it reads from the broker, into an activity intent: publish {\"requestId\":\"1\",\"activity\":\"tv\"} there to try it. The activity controller plans each target and sends msg.theatreCommand; the switch routes each command by its target, and a change node moves it onto that controller's input. Every controller reports msg.theatre target state on output 2, wired straight back to the activity controller, which publishes a complete snapshot through the contract.\n\nName the area on the state contract before deploying: it names the MQTT topics and Home Assistant entities, and until it is set the contract shows \"invalid configuration\". Set the display controller's device hosts and TV pre-shared key too; until then it shows \"invalid configuration\" and the activity waits for it. Wire the controllers' output 1 to your device transports. Activity lighting holds the room's lights through a room lighting controller, which reaches its light through a zigbee2mqtt light node and passes each light's reading back to activity lighting (its third output), so the lights confirm. Set the light's friendly name.\n\nBefore deploying, open the \"your MQTT broker\" config node (any node's Broker setting) and set your broker's host: every node here that talks MQTT subscribes and publishes through it, with no MQTT in, MQTT out or status nodes.",
    "x": 190,
    "y": 40,
    "wires": []
  },
